      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) such that \\( x \\) is a positive multiple of 3 and \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we start by finding the cube root of 1000. The cube root of 1000 is:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{1000} = 10\n\\]\n\nThis means that \\( x^3 < 1000 \\) and \\( x \\leq 10 \\). Since \\( x \\) must be a multiple of 3, we check the multiples of 3 that are less than or equal to 10 and see which one, when cubed, is closest to but less than 1000. The multiples of 3 less than or equal to 10 are 3, 6, and 9. We calculate the cubes of these numbers:\n\n\\[\n3^3 = 27\n\\]\n\\[\n6^3 = 216\n\\]\n\\[\n9^3 = 729\n\\]\n\nThe cube of 9 is 729, which is the largest cube of a multiple of 3 that is less than 1000. Therefore, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
