      "text": "To find the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) such that \\( x \\) is a positive multiple of \\( 3 \\) and \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we start by determining the largest integer \\( x \\) that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, we need to find the cube root of \\( 1000 \\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{1000} = 10\n\\]\nThis tells us that the largest integer \\( x \\) such that \\( x^3 < 1000 \\) is \\( 9 \\) (since \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\) and we need \\( x^3 < 1000 \\)).\n\nNow, we check if \\( x = 9 \\) satisfies the condition that \\( x \\) is a positive multiple of \\( 3 \\):\n\\[\n9 \\div 3 = 3\n\\]\nSince \\( 9 \\) is a multiple of \\( 3 \\), it is indeed a positive multiple of \\( 3 \\). Next, we need to verify that \\( x = 9 \\) satisfies the inequality \\( x^3 < 1000 \\):\n\\[\n9^3 = 9 \\times 9 \\times 9 = 81 \\times 9 = 729\n\\]\nSince \\( 729 < 1000 \\) and \\( 9 \\) is a positive multiple of \\( 3 \\), the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
